<h3 id=3D"1-notification-service" class=3D"font-bold tracking-tight mb-2" s=
tyle=3D"font-size:calc(1.5rem * var(--reading-heading-scale, 1))">1.&nbsp;N=
otification Service</h3><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space=
-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading=
-line-height)">The&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Notification Service</s=
trong>&nbsp;is the&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">entry point</strong>&nb=
sp;for all notification requests, either from external applications or inte=
rnal systems. It exposes&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">APIs</strong>&nbs=
p;that various clients can call to trigger notifications.</p><p class=3D"mb=
-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading=
-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">These could be requests=
 to send&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">transactional notifications</stro=
ng>&nbsp;(e.g., password reset emails),&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">pr=
omotional notifications</strong>&nbsp;(e.g., discount offers), or&nbsp;<str=
ong class=3D"font-bold">system alerts</strong>&nbsp;(e.g., downtime warning=
s).</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"fo=
nt-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">Ea=
ch request is&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">validated</strong>&nbsp;to e=
nsure it contains all the necessary information, such as the recipient=E2=
=80=99s ID, notification type, message content, and channels through which =
the notification should be sent (email, SMS, etc.).</p><p class=3D"mb-4 tex=
t-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-=
size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">For notifications that need t=
o be sent at a future date or time, the Notification Service integrates wit=
h the&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Scheduler</strong>&nbsp;<strong clas=
s=3D"font-bold">Service</strong>.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 ma=
x-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:=
var(--reading-line-height)">After processing the request, the Notification =
Service pushes the notifications to a&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Noti=
fication Queue</strong>&nbsp;(e.g., Kafka or RabbitMQ).</p><h3 id=3D"2-user=
-preference-service" class=3D"font-bold tracking-tight mb-2" style=3D"font-=
size:calc(1.5rem * var(--reading-heading-scale, 1))">2.&nbsp;User Preferenc=
e Service</h3><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" styl=
e=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-heig=
ht)">The&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">User Preference Service</strong>&=
nbsp;allows users to control how they receive notifications.</p><p class=3D=
"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--read=
ing-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">It stores and retrie=
ves individual user preferences for receiving notifications across differen=
t channels.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" sty=
le=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-hei=
ght)">The service tracks which types of notifications users have explicitly=
 opted into or out of.</p><blockquote class=3D"border-l-4 border-primary pl=
-6 py-4 mb-6 bg-muted/50 rounded-r-lg"><p style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-=
font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)"><strong class=3D"font-bo=
ld">Example:</strong>&nbsp;Users may opt out of marketing or promotional co=
ntent</p></blockquote><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y=
-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-l=
ine-height)">To prevent users from being overwhelmed by notifications, the =
User Preference Service enforces&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">frequency=
 limits</strong>&nbsp;for certain types of notifications, especially promot=
ional messages.</p><blockquote class=3D"border-l-4 border-primary pl-6 py-4=
 mb-6 bg-muted/50 rounded-r-lg"><p style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-si=
ze);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Exa=
mple:</strong>&nbsp;A user may only receive 2 promotional notifications per=
 day</p></blockquote><h3 id=3D"3-scheduler-service" class=3D"font-bold trac=
king-tight mb-2" style=3D"font-size:calc(1.5rem * var(--reading-heading-sca=
le, 1))">3.&nbsp;Scheduler Service</h3><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 =
max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-heigh=
t:var(--reading-line-height)">The&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Schedule=
r Service</strong>&nbsp;is responsible for storing and tracking the&nbsp;<s=
trong class=3D"font-bold">scheduled notifications</strong>, notifications t=
hat need to be sent at a specific future time.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-for=
eground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size)=
;line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">These can include reminders, promo=
tional campaigns, or other time-sensitive notifications that are not sent i=
mmediately but must be triggered based on a predefined schedule.</p><blockq=
uote class=3D"border-l-4 border-primary pl-6 py-4 mb-6 bg-muted/50 rounded-=
r-lg"><p style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--read=
ing-line-height)"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Example:</strong>&nbsp;A prom=
otional message might be scheduled for delivery next week.</p></blockquote>=
<p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size=
:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">Once the =
scheduled time arrives, the Scheduler Service pulls the notification from i=
ts storage and sends it to the&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Notificatio=
n Queue</strong>.</p><h3 id=3D"4-notification-queue" class=3D"font-bold tra=
cking-tight mb-2" style=3D"font-size:calc(1.5rem * var(--reading-heading-sc=
ale, 1))">4.&nbsp;Notification Queue</h3><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/9=
0 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-hei=
ght:var(--reading-line-height)">The&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Notifi=
cation Queue&nbsp;</strong>acts as a buffer between the&nbsp;<strong class=
=3D"font-bold">Notification Service</strong>&nbsp;and the&nbsp;<strong clas=
s=3D"font-bold">Channel Processors</strong>.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreg=
round/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);l=
ine-height:var(--reading-line-height)">By decoupling the notification reque=
st submission from the notification delivery, the queue enables the system =
to scale much more effectively, particularly during high-traffic periods.</=
p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-si=
ze:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">The Que=
ue System provides guarantees around message delivery.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 =
text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-fo=
nt-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">Depending on the use case,=
 it can be configured for:</p><div class=3D"ml-4"><ul class=3D"mb-6 space-y=
-2 text-foreground/90 list-outside" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-s=
ize);line-height:var(--reading-line-height);list-style-type:disc;padding-le=
ft:0rem;margin-left:0.5rem"><li class=3D"mb-2"><strong class=3D"font-bold">=
At-least-once delivery</strong>: Ensures that every notification will be se=
nt at least once, even if this results in duplicate messages in rare cases.=
</li><li class=3D"mb-2"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Exactly-once delivery</=
strong>: Ensures that every notification is delivered exactly once, prevent=
ing duplicates while maintaining reliability.</li></ul></div><h3 id=3D"5-ch=
annel-processors" class=3D"font-bold tracking-tight mb-2" style=3D"font-siz=
e:calc(1.5rem * var(--reading-heading-scale, 1))">5.&nbsp;Channel Processor=
s</h3><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"fon=
t-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">The=
&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">Channel Processors&nbsp;</strong>are resp=
onsible for pulling notifications from the&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold"=
>Notification Queue</strong>&nbsp;and delivering them to users via specific=
 channels, such as&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">email</strong>,&nbsp;<s=
trong class=3D"font-bold">SMS</strong>,&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">pu=
sh notifications</strong>, and&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">in-app noti=
fications</strong>.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-=
y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-=
line-height)">By decoupling the Notification Service from the actual delive=
ry, Channel Processors enable&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">independent =
scaling</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">asynchronous pro=
cessing</strong>&nbsp;of notifications.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foreground=
/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line-h=
eight:var(--reading-line-height)">This setup allows each processor to focus=
 on its designated channel, ensuring reliable delivery with built-in retry =
mechanisms and handling failures efficiently.</p><h3 id=3D"6-database-stora=
ge" class=3D"font-bold tracking-tight mb-2" style=3D"font-size:calc(1.5rem =
* var(--reading-heading-scale, 1))">6.&nbsp;Database/Storage</h3><p class=
=3D"mb-4 text-foreground/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--r=
eading-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height)">The&nbsp;<strong =
class=3D"font-bold">Database/Storage</strong>&nbsp;layer manages large volu=
mes of data, including notification content, user preferences, scheduled no=
tifications, delivery logs, and metadata.</p><p class=3D"mb-4 text-foregrou=
nd/90 max-w-4xl space-y-2" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading-font-size);line=
-height:var(--reading-line-height)">The system requires a mix of storage so=
lutions to support various needs:</p><div class=3D"ml-4"><ul class=3D"mb-6 =
space-y-2 text-foreground/90 list-outside" style=3D"font-size:var(--reading=
-font-size);line-height:var(--reading-line-height);list-style-type:disc;pad=
ding-left:0rem;margin-left:0.5rem"><li class=3D"mb-2"><strong class=3D"font=
-bold">Transactional Data</strong>: A relational database like&nbsp;<strong=
 class=3D"font-bold">PostgreSQL</strong>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font=
-bold">MySQL</strong>&nbsp;stores structured data, such as notification log=
s and delivery status.</li><li class=3D"mb-2"><strong class=3D"font-bold">U=
ser Preferences</strong>:&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">NoSQL databases<=
/strong>&nbsp;(e.g., DynamoDB, MongoDB) store large volumes of user-specifi=
c data, such as preferences and rate limits.</li><li class=3D"mb-2"><strong=
 class=3D"font-bold">Blob Storage</strong>: For notifications containing la=
rge attachments (e.g., email with images or PDFs),&nbsp;<strong class=3D"fo=
nt-bold">Amazon</strong>&nbsp;<strong class=3D"font-bold">S3</strong>&nbsp;=
or similar services can store them.</li></ul></div><div data-orientation=3D=
